WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.590
- [Announcer] Here goes Cosmic Girl.

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.780
- [Narrator] This might look
like a normal airplane flight.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.150
- [Announcer] And yes, we are wheels up.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.530
- [Narrator] But once it's in the sky,

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.833
it's clear it's actually a rocket launch.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.090
Richard Branson's Virgin
Orbit made a big bet

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:18.630
on launching satellites
from Airborne 747s.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:20.550
It was expected to be a cheaper

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.871
and more flexible method than
traditional Rocket launches.

00:00:23.871 --> 00:00:26.097
- [Announcer] And liftoff of Starlink 2-A.

00:00:26.097 --> 00:00:28.590
- [Narrator] But that bet hasn't paid off.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:32.790
In March, Virgin Orbit
laid off 85% of its staff

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.100
and filed for bankruptcy in April.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.710
- Our runway was set and
our runway was shorter

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.900
than perhaps we needed.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.823
- [Narrator] So, what went wrong?

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.820
Virgin Orbit started out as
a branch of Richard Branson's

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.930
larger space venture,
Virgin Galactic, in 2012.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:56.850
- Richard Branson as has
Elon Musk, as has Jeff Bezos,

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.050
talks about space as an aspirational goal,

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.060
as something about which he's passionate

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.320
and believes is important.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.420
- [Narrator] Carissa
Christensen is an analyst

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.720
who has been working in the
space industry for 30 years.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.380
- That aspirational
vision is an unusual part

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.660
of this business compared
to an awful lot of others.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.180
- [Narrator] In 2012,
there was a growing market

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.590
for small satellites.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:20.970
They're typically cheaper

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.550
and easier to build than larger ones.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.890
But launching them was another story.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.340
Most had to hitch a ride on
larger conventional launches

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.140
which were difficult to come by

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.320
and sometimes didn't drop the
satellite in the right place.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.180
So while Virgin was developing

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.610
its spacecraft for human launches,

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.310
it saw another opportunity.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.380
- Say hello to Launcher One.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.960
- [Narrator] Launcher One
was a small launch vehicle

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.230
that Virgin positioned as
a small satellite solution.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:50.640
Small launch vehicles

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.100
were a fairly small industry at the time.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.560
Launcher One was one of two new vehicles

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.080
that went into development in 2012.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.270
- The investment thesis being that,

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.990
those small satellites
would value the opportunity

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.240
to be launched on a smaller launcher

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.250
because they could go where they wanted,

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.910
when they wanted and the total
launch cost would be lower.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.530
- [Narrator] As Branson told CNN,

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:16.860
- The current cost's about
$40 million, $30, $40 million.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.500
We'll be able to do it
for less than $10 million.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.150
- [Narrator] Virgin
planned to achieve this

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.850
using that unique method
of putting its rockets

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:27.000
on modified Boeing 747s.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.150
In typical launches,
rockets need one of these,

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.370
a powerful stage one booster

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:34.680
to send it straight up into the atmosphere

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.383
before smaller stage
two engines take over.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.760
Using a jet presented a
faster, more flexible,

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.210
and reusable way to get
Launcher One closer to orbit

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.550
before detaching to finish the trip.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.080
No launchpad required.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:50.730
- The idea that you could launch

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:54.240
from anywhere and that
you would have flexibility

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.570
with regard to when you
launched was very attractive.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:00.750
And because you're not
competing for launch sites

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:04.440
that was the thought that Virgin
could provide that service.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.280
- [Narrator] Then in 2017,
Orbit spun off of Galactic,

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.760
with former Boeing executive
Dan Hart at the helm.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.530
But there was an issue.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.320
It still hadn't had a single launch

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.320
and competition in the space was growing.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.340
In 2020, the company made
its first launch attempt

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.320
but it wasn't successful.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.630
- Some disappointment that we didn't get

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:30.510
to finish the flight and take it to orbit

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.280
but we were all prepared for that.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.010
We collected an enormous amount of data

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.390
- [Narrator] To bring in revenue,

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.330
the company needed to be
making regular launches.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:43.140
In the meantime, Virgin Orbit
was burning through cash.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.850
Analysts estimated the company

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:49.020
had spent more than 400 million
developing its launch system

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.180
and it looked to raise more.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:55.110
Then in January, 2021, success.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:56.820
- [Announcer] Launcher One's in space.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:57.690
- [Narrator] The company deployed

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.110
10 small satellites into
orbit for the first time.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.470
It made its first
commercial launch in June.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:05.820
- I think Orbit right now are gonna

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.820
concentrate on satellites
into orbit and you know,

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.750
and just ramp up, you know,
ramp up their capabilities

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.670
- [Narrator] And ramp up their finances.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.280
Virgin Orbit decided to
go public through a merger

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:23.850
with a special purpose
acquisition company or SPAC.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:27.240
- An earlier stage
company trading publicly

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.320
has a level of scrutiny and
visibility into what it's doing

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:35.610
that's very challenging for
a company at that stage.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.230
- [Narrator] But the
listing didn't generate

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:40.740
the 483 million Virgin
Orbit was looking for.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.490
Instead, it ended up
with less than half that.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:47.700
While Virgin continued
its launches in 2022,

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.640
larger launches were
becoming more accessible.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.730
New ride share options
like one with SpaceX,

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.980
catered to small satellite companies,

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:58.320
and advances in technology made it easier

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.450
to drop satellites in the right spot

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.370
unlike earlier large launches.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:05.250
- It really is the taxi versus a bus.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.790
If you take a taxi,
you'll pay a modest amount

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.040
compared to hiring a whole bus.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.870
But if you just buy one seat on the bus

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.970
it's cheaper than taking the taxi.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.580
- [Narrator] But Orbit still
had one major relationship

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.820
it could potentially lean
on to bring in more funding,

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.830
the UK Space Agency.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:26.670
- A government entity, is a
good customer for small launch.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.500
And it was important for the UK

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:32.310
because the UK has been
expanding its presence in space

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:35.700
and was looking for an opportunity

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:38.070
to conduct launch from the UK.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:39.300
- [Narrator] So in January,

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:43.290
it planned the first ever
launch to take place on UK soil,

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.369
but it didn't go as planned.

00:05:45.369 --> 00:05:48.330
- It appears that Launcher
One has suffered an anomaly

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:51.030
which will prevent us from
making orbit for this mission.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:52.800
- [Narrator] The failure
caused Orbit stock

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.880
to fall 14% that day and
it continued to slide.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.690
- Investors are just not as risk tolerant.

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:02.700
There's not much capital available.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.820
There are other places
for that capital to go.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.790
And so, particularly for a launch business

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:13.530
which needs a lot of capital,
that becomes a problem.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:16.620
- [Narrator] Virgin Orbit's
layoffs and bankruptcy filing

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.020
followed in the wake
of that failed launch,

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.600
- The satellite launch firm
tied to British billionaire,

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.090
Richard Branson, failed to
secure the funding needed

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:25.110
to keep operating.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:26.880
- My point is, in this environment,

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.570
any execution failures are
just gonna be magnified.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:31.860
- [Narrator] CEO, Dan Hart,

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.570
said in a statement at the time

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:36.810
that the company must do what
is best for the business.

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:40.260
Virgin Orbit declined to
comment further for this video.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.800
But this isn't necessarily
the end for the company.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.290
Virgin Orbit is hoping to find an investor

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.000
to buy them out of bankruptcy.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.940
- I'm optimistic that will find a partner

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.500
who wants to take us forward.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:53.460
- [Narrator] And for now,

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.052
the company is still
planning more launches.

00:06:56.052 --> 00:06:58.802
(cheerful music)

